Generative Models 

Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) 
Consist of two networks—a generator and a discriminator—that compete. The generator creates data, and the discriminator evaluates it, leading to highly realistic outputs. 

Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) 
Encode input data into a compressed representation and then decode it back, learning to generate new data similar to the input. 

Autoregressive Models 
Predict the next element in a sequence based on previous elements. Used in language modeling and time series forecasting. 

GPT (Generative Pretrained Transformer) 
A type of autoregressive model based on the transformer architecture. It generates coherent and context-aware text by predicting the next token in a sequence. 

LLMs (Large Language Models) 

Advanced generative models trained on massive text datasets. Built on transformer architecture, they can perform a wide range of tasks such as answering questions, summarizing, translating, and generating text. 

Examples: GPT-3, GPT-4, PaLM, LLaMA, Claude 

 

1. Self-Attention Mechanism 

What it is: 
A technique that allows a model to focus on different parts of a sentence when processing each word. 

Simple analogy: 
Imagine reading a sentence like: 
"The cat sat on the mat because it was tired." 
To understand what "it" refers to, your brain looks back at "cat." 
Self-attention does the same — it helps the model decide which words are important to look at when understanding or generating text. 

Why it matters in LLMs: 
It enables models to understand context and relationships between words, even if they’re far apart in a sentence. 

 

2. Embedding 

What it is: 
A way to convert words or sentences into numbers (vectors) that capture their meaning. 

Simple analogy: 
Think of embedding as placing words on a map. Words with similar meanings (like "king" and "queen") are placed close together. 

Why it matters in LLMs: 
Embeddings allow models to compare meanings, find similarities, and understand context — not just match exact words. 

 

3. Transformer Model 

What it is: 
A type of neural network architecture that uses self-attention to process and generate text. 

Simple analogy: 
Imagine a smart assistant that reads a paragraph and instantly knows which parts are important, how they relate, and what to say next — that’s what a transformer does. 

Why it matters in LLMs: 
Transformers are the backbone of modern LLMs. They allow models to handle long-range dependencies, generate coherent text, and scale efficiently. 

 

4. Neural Network 

What it is: 
A system of interconnected "neurons" (mathematical functions) that learn patterns from data. 

Simple analogy: 
Like a brain made of math — it learns by adjusting connections based on examples. 

Why it matters in LLMs: 
Neural networks are the foundation of all deep learning models, including transformers. They learn how to predict the next word, answer questions, and more. 

 

 

 

 

